motion wrapped into reusable, parameterized functions — the vocabulary of clean code. a function is a named behavior: define square(size) once, call it anywhere, change it in one place. connect backwards: functions are to code what loops were to repeated blocks in grade 7 — the next rung on the same ladder (don’t repeat yourself). the parameter is the power move: square(20), square(40) — one definition, infinite squares. build a small personal library today: square(size), polygon(n, size), celebrate() (leds + sound). this library literally gets imported into the capstone — tell them that now, so “write it well” has a reason. naming matters: celebrate() reads like english; func2() reads like regret. live-code square() together; call it three times with different sizes. pairs build polygon(n, size) — the 360/n rule becomes one line of math in code. design celebrate() freely: lights + sound + a wiggle; must be a single call. library review: swap code with another pair; can they use your functions without asking you? the another-pair-can-use-it test is the real definition of a good function. watch for logic vs. syntax confusion: polygon(3, ...) turning 120° is correct — remind them of the exterior angle. polygon(n, size) draws at least three different shapes celebrate() runs as a single call another pair used the library without explanations import mbot2, cyberpi

def polygon(n, size):
    for i in range(n):
        mbot2.straight(size)
        mbot2.turn(360 / n)

def celebrate():
    cyberpi.led.on(255, 180, 0)
    cyberpi.audio.play('yay')

polygon(5, 25)   # pentagon, one line
celebrate() a function library another pair can use cold, with no explanation offered and no questions answered. another pair used the library cold, with no explanation every function carries a one-line comment aimed at a future reader

grade 7’s wander rebuilt in python on a sensor it never had — then refactored into clean functions. full control flow: if / elif / else and while. the vehicle is deliberately nostalgic — rewrite grade 7’s wander mode in python. but grade 7’s robot escaped from a distance, and this one has no ultrasonic, so the same behaviour has to be built on a different sense: a taped boundary on the floor, and the quad rgb probes that see it. say that out loud as the day’s real lesson — the behaviour is not the sensor. roam, notice the edge, back off, turn away, roam again: that shape is unchanged since grade 7, and only one line of it knows what a sensor is. then introduce refactoring: improving code’s shape without changing its behaviour. extract the escape manoeuvre into escape(), extract the sensing into on_edge(), name the magic numbers as constants (back_cm = 10), and re-run — identical robot behaviour, radically clearer code. then land the point: because the sensing lives in on_edge(), swapping the robot’s eyes again would touch two lines. that “same behaviour, better shape” discipline is the heart of code quality, and it is a rubric line in the capstone code review. rewrite wander in python: while true + edge check + escape. refactor: extract on_edge() and escape(), name constants, add two comments that say why not what. arena test: does refactored wander behave identically? (it must — that’s the definition.) on your lesson sheet: before/after code snippets — which would you rather debug at 11 pm? random.randint needs the import — a good ‘read the error’ moment. ‘why not what’ comments: # far enough to clear the tape, not # set back_cm to 10. if the robot reverses onto the boundary and sticks there, back_cm is smaller than the wheelbase behind the probes. measure it rather than guessing. tape the boundary wide — a thin line at speed is crossed between two sensor reads, and that is a genuine sampling lesson, not a bug. python wander roams the arena and turns back at the boundary reliably on_edge() and escape() extracted; constants named; behaviour unchanged comments explain why, not what import mbot2, cyberpi, random

cruise  = 40    # rpm: quick, but stoppable
back_cm = 10    # far enough to clear tape

def sees(probe):
    return cyberpi.quad_rgb_sensor.is_color(
        'black', probe)

def on_edge():
    # no ultrasonic: the edge is a colour
    return sees('l1') or sees('r1')

def escape():
    mbot2.straight(-back_cm)
    mbot2.turn(random.randint(60, 120))

while true:
    if on_edge():
        escape()
    else:
        mbot2.drive_speed(cruise, cruise) refactored code whose behaviour is proved unchanged by measurement rather than asserted by the people who changed it. behaviour proved unchanged by measurement rather than by claim no magic numbers survive anywhere in the file

the mission code restructured around a main loop + state variable — and paper-traced first. session 13’s pipeline was a straight script — fine until something fails mid-mission and the program can’t express ‘go back and retry’. introduce the state machine pattern: a state variable (searching / approaching / picking / delivering / done), a main loop that reads sensors and, depending on the current state, acts and decides transitions. it’s grade 8’s mood variable wearing a hard hat — say exactly that; the recognition lands. before running: paper trace. one teammate is the cpu: finger on the current state, walk through a scenario (‘marker seen → state becomes picking’), including the nasty ones (‘what if the grip fails in picking?’ → they’ll want a retry transition — let them invent it). tracing catches design holes in minutes that debugging would find in hours; it is also precisely how they’ll explain their code in the capstone review. board the state diagram for the s13 mission; agree states and transitions as a class. paper trace in pairs, including two failure scenarios; patch the diagram where it breaks. restructure the code to the state pattern; behavior should match s13’s (refactor discipline!). on your lesson sheet: your state diagram, photographed or redrawn. states are nouns-in-progress, transitions are events — keep the diagram honest. if the trace can’t answer ‘what happens if x’, the code can’t either; fix the diagram first. state diagram covers the mission incl. one retry path paper trace survived two failure scenarios restructured code matches s13 behavior state = 'searching'

while state != 'done':
    if state == 'searching':
        follow_line_step()
        if see_marker():
            state = 'picking'
    elif state == 'picking':
        if pick_ok():
            state = 'delivering'
        else:
            state = 'searching'  # retry!
    elif state == 'delivering':
        carry_to(40); place()
        state = 'done' a state diagram that survives two failure scenarios on paper, and restructured code whose behaviour still matches session 13. two failure scenarios traced on paper before any code was changed restructured behaviour still matches session 13's, measured not assumed
